      "modelAnswer": "<span>The pancreas monitors blood glucose concentration and maintains it within a narrow range by releasing two antagonistic hormones. When blood glucose levels are high, such as after eating, the pancreas secretes insulin. Insulin facilitates the uptake of glucose by cells and stimulates the liver to convert glucose into glycogen for storage, thereby lowering blood glucose concentration. Conversely, when glucose levels fall too low, the pancreas releases glucagon. Glucagon signals the liver to break down glycogen into glucose and release it into the bloodstream, raising blood glucose concentration. This coordinated action helps maintain glucose homeostasis in the body.</span>",
